# ShadeAuto hub characterization log, 2026-10-23, shade 12177 ("Kitchen")
#
# One 200 s probe of the same hub as hub-characterization-2026-10-22.log (NienMadeHub,
# firmware 6.1.25). It was produced by a one-off standard-library script, not by
# tools/hubwatch.py. The script:
#   - called /registration for the ThingName;
#   - held POST /NM/v1/notification open with http.client (request body
#     {"ThingName": <ThingName>, "Timestamp": <now>, "Timeout": 300}), read the response
#     with read1() as bytes arrived and split it into JSON objects with
#     json.JSONDecoder().raw_decode; each object is an EVENT line, printed when it arrived;
#   - polled /status about once a second and printed shade 12177's BottomRailPosition,
#     TargetBottomRailPosition, MiddleRailPosition, TargetMiddleRailPosition and per-shade
#     Timestamp (STATUS initial), then every change of them (STATUS change, as old/new pairs);
#   - about 8 s in, sent one /control {"PeripheralUID": 12177, "StatusRequest": 0} plus
#     TaskID, Timestamp and ThingName (the CONTROL line shows the hub's reply).
# tools/hubwatch.py --notify now reads the stream the same way.
#
# Times are the client's wall clock, America/Detroit (EDT, UTC-4); "+N s" is seconds since
# the probe started. "Status" in an EVENT and "Timestamp" in the /control reply are the hub's
# clock in milliseconds; the per-shade Timestamp in STATUS lines is the hub's clock in seconds.
# The vane move to 55 at 04:39 was not sent by the probe: it was made in the ShadeAuto app
# (session notes, not machine-logged). The stream was opened once and never ended or failed
# during the probe. No ThingName appears below.

